WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.050
- Ooh sausage! (crew person laughs)

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.225
Oh (beep) we're rolling.

00:00:04.225 --> 00:00:07.225
(upbeat horn music)

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.200
Welcome back to Tasty's Making It Big.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.820
I'm Alvin and I make large versions

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.570
of some of America's most beloved foods.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.160
And today we are going to
be making a huge version

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.730
of the classic McDonald's McGriddle.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.170
I actually grew up never
eating one of these,

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.020
I was given hash browns and
orange juice by my parents

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.210
because they were healthier, they said.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.540
That's definitely not true!

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.190
You know how much fat is in those things?

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.370
I'm going to relive my childhood
nostalgia or lack thereof

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.310
by making a large McGriddle!

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.163
Enough said, let's get started.

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:43.790
(jazzy bass music)

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:44.623
As I've been told,

00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:47.790
a McGriddle starts with
a pancake like bun,

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.270
so we're going to make giant pancake.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.400
This may or may not be the same way

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.060
that they do make it at the golden arches,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.370
if they do let me know,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.071
I'm actually curious of how they're made.

00:00:59.071 --> 00:01:00.710
(forcefully whisking liquids)

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:04.100
Milk and I'm spilling, good job, dude.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.120
Flour, baking soda, sugar and salt

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.963
and golden whisk for the golden arches.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.990
Good one, Alvin.
(upbeat jazz music)

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.103
Don't want too many lumps in here.

00:01:18.595 --> 00:01:21.700
(wet ingredients slopping together)

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.880
Whoo!

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.680
All right, let's get in this butter.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.770
Double whisk, double whisk. (laughs)

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.280
It's like rubbing your
belly and patting your head.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.683
I can do that actually.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:37.583
Good job. (audience clapping)

00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:39.010
I wonder if there's like a life scenario

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.250
in which that'll actually come in handy.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.630
I don't know, maybe like squid
game or something like that.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.987
Batter's looking good.
Nice and battered.

00:01:45.987 --> 00:01:47.970
(metal whisks clanging on metal bowl)

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:52.967
And this is going into
quite a large pancake pan,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:57.793
it's over there, see you
guys like 20 feet from here.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.580
So I forgot to add something.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.900
The McGriddle apparently

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.563
has a lot of syrup infused into the bun.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:11.090
That's pretty, man, probably
just poured in like $30

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.600
into this bun right here.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.984
I've been informed that this batter

00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:16.710
is the same amount of batter

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.190
used to make around 60 pancakes,

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.310
and this is only one of the buns,

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.960
so this McGriddle is gonna be

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.960
approximately 120 times the
size of a normal one! (laughs)

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.990
In here we have quite a large pan,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.350
pancakes should go in a hot pan,

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.633
this has been roughly preheated.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.520
Yeah, there we go, nice sizzle.
That's good, that's good!

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.310
Grandpa told me, try not
to waste, so I won't.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.800
Do my best grandpa.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.800
Back in you go, dude, this
will cook until it's ready.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.561
I'm going to make a big sausage thing now!

00:02:57.561 --> 00:02:59.540
(upbeat jazzy bass)

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.810
What is under this bowl, you might ask?

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:07.810
Well, we have approximately
20 pounds of ground pork.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.750
This is a lot of meat

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.490
and we're going to make
the sausage out of this

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.740
and to do this, we're going
to need a lot of seasoning.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:16.573
Lots of seasonings,

00:03:16.573 --> 00:03:21.450
garlic, onion powder, ground
sage, thyme, a lot of thyme,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.017
salt, pepper and brown sugar.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.210
Honestly, I think sugar and
savory foods are underrated

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.230
and often missed, but they
can make the big difference

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.470
between what tastes really
good and what tastes just okay.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:45.470
Just like salt in desserts
is also quite underrated.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.490
This is probably also why
the McGriddle tastes great

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:50.460
because there's syrup in the bun,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.670
there's sugar in the patty,
there's probably sugar

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.320
in a lot of other things as well.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.630
So ground pork, the one we have,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.600
has a good amount of fat content

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.060
and that's one of the reasons
why sausage tastes good,

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.880
but this sausage is as mixed as it can.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.420
Honestly, it does smell good.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.420
All right, we gotta get
you into our big cake pan.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.682
Do not handle your newborn like this.

00:04:15.682 --> 00:04:18.330
(meat slaps onto pan)
(baby cries)

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.113
Yeah, definitely don't handle
your newborn like this.

00:04:21.113 --> 00:04:22.160
(exaggerated grunting)

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.770
Who needs a rumble when you got this?

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.230
Fun fact, I'm actually pretty
good at giving massages

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.650
because growing up my
parents would always ask me

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:33.560
to give them a massage
at the end of the day,

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.860
because they were tired and
overtime I got quite good

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.810
at knowing how to massage correctly.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.449
Some people think, "Oh, you just?"

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:42.550
(exaggerated pounding)

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:43.970
No, you can't do that.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:45.550
You actually have to go like this

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.810
and you don't make your fingers firm,

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.443
you have to let your fingers be loose.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.960
And a massage is more
than just doing this,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.030
you got to do it the right way,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.140
because if you hit the
wrong spots, it hurts!

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:03.023
So I'm giving this sausage
patty a little bit of massage.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.168
All right, into the oven we go!

00:05:09.168 --> 00:05:10.933
(pained grunt) Oh, it's heavy!

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:15.083
Somebody order a pizza? Whoo!

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.460
Oh, what's up, you little pancake?

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.670
Here you have a buddy to keep
you company in that oven.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.190
Oh man, it's looking good.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.147
All right, peace out. Have
a good time, be friends.

00:05:26.147 --> 00:05:27.900
(upbeat jazz music)

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.430
A McGriddle has signature
fluffy egg square,

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.700
and we are going to make the
same exact thing, but big.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.832
So 24 eggs gonna get destroyed!

00:05:39.832 --> 00:05:44.309
(immersion blender whirring)

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:48.777
Die! Die you yolks!
(energetic heavy metal music)

00:05:48.777 --> 00:05:50.694
(beep)

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:53.620
Ooh, that was fun.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.460
Heavy cream, make it fluffy, and salt.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.983
One more time into this machine of death!

00:05:59.983 --> 00:06:02.306
(immersion blender whirring)

00:06:02.306 --> 00:06:03.306
I like this.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.570
Oh, all right, thank you!

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:11.410
Honestly, one of the most
underrated tools in the kitchen

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:14.300
the immersion blender
solves a lot of problems,

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:17.510
turns them into smooth solutions.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.510
Into a tray, this is 24 eggs,

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:22.060
we're gonna be making
two of these because,

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.922
well this is not making it average,

00:06:23.922 --> 00:06:26.290
(upbeat horn music)

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:27.293
gotta make it big!

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:33.660
Into some parchment, make my
life easier for the removal.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.804
Into the oven we go, yet again!

00:06:35.804 --> 00:06:37.870
(upbeat jazz music)

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.730
So, the fourth and final component

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.940
that we have for our McGriddle today

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.330
is the giant cheese slice.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:47.680
It could be easy to throw cheese
slices on top of the thing

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.220
and call it a day, but this
isn't, you know, Making It Lazy.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.660
(upbeat horn music)

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:56.190
So we're going to make our own version

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.630
of an American cheese slice,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:02.630
and that starts with gelatin
and milk, getting bloomed here.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.500
You might recall this technique being used

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:09.640
from the previous season where
we made a giant Krabby Patty.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.940
And if you did, you would
be right, 10 points to you.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:15.130
While the gelatin blooms,

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:18.687
we're going to heat up
some milk and some butter.

00:07:18.687 --> 00:07:22.300
This is a nice glass,
where'd you get this glass?

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:23.960
This glass may or may not disappear

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.040
within the course of the next three days.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.223
Some butter, in goes the
gelatin-bloomed mixture.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.660
I believe I've seen this
method used on other websites

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.240
and people that are really good with food,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.770
such as Serious Eats, J. Kenji López-Alt.

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.690
Really cool technique to make you own

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:44.510
American style cheese with other cheeses,

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:45.780
which makes a lot of sense,

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.190
and I like burgers, so if I
wanted to make a fancy burger,

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:50.573
I'll probably do this.

00:07:50.573 --> 00:07:53.670
This is more cheddar than
I have in my bank account,

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.794
that's okay, we'll talk about that later.

00:07:55.794 --> 00:07:58.590
(metal tool tapping against glass)

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.763
Some salt. (blender whirring)

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.130
I'm very prone to spilling,

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:09.860
so we're going to actually
not do something stupid!

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.579
We'll make the smart decision.
(countdown timer ticking)

00:08:11.579 --> 00:08:13.630
(blender whirs) (plastic snaps)

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:17.270
Oh! No, it's good, I'll go the other way.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.723
As you can see, I'm
very prone to spilling.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.793
Yeah.
(machine mixing liquids)

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.982
That's a sound!

00:08:29.982 --> 00:08:32.950
I'll get in there and scrape
down the sides real quick.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:35.910
Ooh! That's looking nice, you
guys see that? (small laugh)

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:39.670
I'll scrape down the side.
That is satisfying, wow!

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:43.713
Now gotta get it on to here.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.740
Look at that, that's some
thick, smooth cheese!

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.010
You guys ever heard of Velveeta?

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.110
Or the nacho cheese you
get at the bowling alley

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:54.420
with your chips?

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:55.690
Those are good.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:00.630
Spread this guy. Ooh, ooh! That's nice.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:02.100
Ooh, that's nice, I like that a lot.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.370
Oh, so smooth! Damn!

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.160
It's like frosting a cake,

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:10.033
but there's no cake, frosting is cheese,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.840
so it's actually not like
frosting a cake at all,

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:16.460
it's just really satisfying to do.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.420
It's also kind of like a race against time

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:19.750
because you've got to get it

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.230
before the cheese starts to set,

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:25.893
but it's feeling pretty good,
looking really good! Whoo!

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.603
All right, I'm gonna call it.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:34.530
This is gonna be our large cheese slice.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:38.440
And this is going in to the fridge.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:41.760
(upbeat jazz music)
(Alvin pained grunting)

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.010
I think it's time we can
build this giant McGriddle.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:50.850
We got a bun, we got a patty,

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.670
and we have two sheets of
eggs, two sheets of cheese,

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.930
with a little alley-oop
from the culinary team,

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:04.040
I think it's time we build.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:05.740
Let's see how this bun looks like.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:08.232
Wait (scoffs) it's going to flip

00:10:08.232 --> 00:10:09.650
and it's going to hit me in the face.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:10.483
Here we go.

00:10:12.537 --> 00:10:16.579
(laughs) Hold on, my brain
can't work right now.

00:10:16.579 --> 00:10:17.440
(pan slamming onto cutting board)

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.883
Whoo! Ooh, that's a nice one.

00:10:20.883 --> 00:10:23.640
Look at that, it's all shiny and nice.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:25.486
Okay, so this'll be our top bun.

00:10:25.486 --> 00:10:28.817
(energetic jazz music)

00:10:28.817 --> 00:10:32.730
All right, no bun
crackage, that's all good.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.450
So you got to get the cheese slices out.

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:37.103
Let's give a little trim first.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.383
Now let's get this guy on there.

00:10:45.975 --> 00:10:49.610
There we go, now that's a
cheese slice! (chuckles)

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:53.968
Okay, this is a heat gun, it
helps melt things on this show.

00:10:53.968 --> 00:10:55.170
(heat gun fan blowing)

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:56.600
Ooh, that's hot.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.329
We'll be right back. (laughs)

00:10:58.329 --> 00:11:00.420
(triumphant horn music)

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.270
It's getting a little melty!
(exaggerated sniffing)

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:04.410
It does smell good.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:06.970
I'm also counting on
the heat from this patty

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:10.133
to continue melting it, so here we go.

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:14.640
Whoo! Oh boy, time for the eggs.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.673
Whoo! That's one, this is two.

00:11:20.579 --> 00:11:23.110
Oh man, that's pretty cool.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:26.593
All right, and the final shiny top bun.

00:11:27.533 --> 00:11:28.640
I got it, yeah.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.223
This is sturdy, nice!

00:11:35.682 --> 00:11:38.640
Ooh, that's a McGriddle! (laughs)

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:41.390
While McDonald's seems
to have their signature

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:43.620
on every single McGriddle,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.660
well, you know, I like
to put my name on things,

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:50.200
so I want to see if we
can dot in a little A.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:55.200
Get nice pattern going it.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:59.866
Look at that, that's
an A, or that's a line.

00:11:59.866 --> 00:12:01.270
Oh, this is going to be delicious.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:03.300
Look how much syrup we're
putting in this thing.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.463
Nobody tell my doctor.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.920
Just kidding, he'll
going to see the video.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:11.430
Oh, you could see it in
the top down! Let's go!

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:15.620
This is not a Mc-Griddle,
this is A-Griddle,

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.420
'cause there's A on it!

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:22.420
This is a normal McGriddle,
this is A-Griddle.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:26.600
As you can see, there's
quite a big size difference.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.903
Okay, let's bisect this
guy and see what we've got.

00:12:29.903 --> 00:12:32.239
Here we go, straight down the middle.

00:12:32.239 --> 00:12:35.406
(exaggerated huffing)

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.160
Whoo! Oh, heck yeah.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:43.210
Pardon me while I don
my protective measures,

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:44.103
and here we go!

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:49.740
Whoo! That cross section!

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.550
A double egg layer, some nicely
cooked sausage, still juicy,

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.593
and the cheese!

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:57.400
That's pretty cool.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.423
All right, I'll take a honorary wedge.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:03.900
One slice is bigger than the whole thing.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.314
Cheers.

00:13:05.314 --> 00:13:07.410
Okay, I'm gonna have to eat
this in like three bites

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.428
to get all the flavor.

00:13:09.428 --> 00:13:12.845
(whimsical string music)

00:13:15.328 --> 00:13:17.678
(speaking with mouthful)
Better than I thought!

00:13:19.613 --> 00:13:23.387
I think the key to this
whole thing is the syrup.

00:13:23.387 --> 00:13:26.260
The syrup is what makes
it taste and smell like

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:28.150
what I think this tastes like.

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.625
Wait, I haven't had this one, hold on.

00:13:30.625 --> 00:13:34.940
(laughs) It's like the
same thing, but big!

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:36.590
Also take a little wedge of this.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:38.613
Let's see.

00:13:42.396 --> 00:13:44.440
(speaking with mouthful)
This tastes so similar!

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:48.440
This is the same thing.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:50.100
That's really cool!
(energetic horn music)

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:51.963
Good job, Alvin.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:54.510
Well, that was quite fun.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:57.410
It's a good thing that I
do like eating what I make,

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.400
because it seems like I'll be having

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:03.570
lunch, breakfast and dinner
for quite a long time.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:07.120
I can feel all the sugar
getting to my blood vessels

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.970
and I haven't had this much
sugar in quite a while,

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.170
so I'm going to go and maybe run it off.

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:15.278
I'll see you guys in
the next episode, peace!

00:14:15.278 --> 00:14:19.111
(energetic horn music swells)

